Attention is the customs officer at the border between the world and your mind.

Everything is trying to cross. Attention decides what gets stamped in.

And we “pay” attention because it costs something real: time, working memory, metabolic energy, emotional bandwidth, opportunity. To attend to one thing is to neglect a thousand others.

So attention is also a kind of tax collector.

You pay in:

* moments you can’t get back,
* alternative thoughts you didn’t think,
* possibilities you didn’t pursue,
* emotional weight you assigned,
* memory you strengthened.

That’s why “pay attention” is such a revealing phrase. Attention isn’t free observation. It is allocation.

And if we personify it further:

Attention is the banker of reality.

Whatever you keep funding gets richer in your internal world.

Fear compounds.
Love compounds.
Resentment compounds.
Skill compounds.
Faith compounds.
Desire compounds.

So the dangerous question is not merely what has my attention?

It’s:

Who convinced me this deserved my money?

Because once something learns how to invoice your attention, it can start living rent-free in the mind.

And maybe that’s why attention feels almost sacred: it is the one currency every mind must spend, but can never earn more of retroactively.

You don’t just pay attention.

You pay with yourself.